Provably Efficient Maximum Entropy Exploration\line Elad Hazan1 2 Sham M. Kakade1 3 4 Karan Singh1 2 Abby Van Soest2}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
1 Google AI Princeton\line 2 Department of Computer Science, Princeton University}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
3 Allen School of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Washington\line 4 Department of Statistics, University of Washington}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
\{ehazan,karans,asoest\}@princeton.edu, sham@cs.washington.edu}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
Abstract\line Suppose an agent is in a (possibly unknown) Markov Decision Process in the absence of a reward}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
signal, what might we hope that an agent can efficiently learn to do? This work studies a broad class of\line objectives that are defined solely as functions of the state-visitation frequencies that are induced by how\line the agent behaves. For example, one natural, intrinsically defined, objective problem is for the agent to\line learn a policy which induces a distribution over state space that is as uniform as possible, which can be\line measured in an entropic sense. We provide an efficient algorithm to optimize such such intrinsically defined\line objectives, when given access to a black box planning oracle (which is robust to function approximation).\line Furthermore, when restricted to the tabular setting where we have sample based access to the MDP, our\line proposed algorithm is provably efficient, both in terms of its sample and computational complexities.\line Key to our algorithmic methodology is utilizing the conditional gradient method (a.k.a. the Frank-Wolfe\line algorithm) which utilizes an approximate MDP solver.}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
1 Introduction\line A fundamental problem in reinforcement learning is that of exploring the state space. How do we understand\line what is even possible in a given environment without a reward signal?}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
This question has received a lot of attention, with approaches such as learning with intrinsic reward and\line curiosity driven methods, surveyed below. Our work studies a class of objectives that is defined solely as\line function of the state-visitation frequencies. A natural such objective is finding a policy that maximizes the\line entropy of the induced distribution over the state space. More generally, our approach extends to any concave\line function over distributions.}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
Suppose the MDP is fully and precisely known, in terms of states, actions, and the entire transition matrix.\line Then maximizing the entropy can be recast as a convex optimization problem (see Section 3.2.2 or [DFVR03])\line over the space of state-visitation frequencies induced by the exhaustive set of all policies. However, most RL\line instances that are common in practice exhibit at least one of several complications:}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{
\u8212\'97 }{\loch
prohibitively large state space (i.e. Chess or Go)\line \u8212\'97 unknown transition matrix (as in common Atari games)\line These scenarios often require function approximation, ie. restricting the search to a non-linearly parameterized\line policy class (eg. neural networks), which makes the entropy maximization problem non-convex.}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
As a remedy for the computational difficulty, we propose considering an approximate planning oracle: an\line efficient method that given a well-specified reward signal can find an optimizing policy. Such sample-based\line planning oracles have been empirically observed to work well with non-linear policy classes. Given such an\line oracle, we give a provably efficient method for exploration based on the conditional gradient (or Frank-Wolfe)\line algorithm [FW56].}
