\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
1.2 Related work\line We review related works in this section.}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
Reward Shaping & Imitation Learning: Direct optimization approaches to RL (such as policy\line gradient methods) tend to perform favorably when random sequences of actions lead the agent to some\line positive reward, but tend to fail when the rewards are sparse or myopic. Thus far, the most practical\line approaches to address this have either been through some carefully constructed reward shaping (e.g. [NHR99]\line where dense reward functions are provided to make the optimization problem more tractable) or through\line inverse reinforcement learning and imitation learning [AN04, RGB11] (where an expert demonstrates to the\line agent how to act).}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
PAC Learning: For the case of tabular Markov decision processes, the balance of exploration and\line exploitation has been addressed in that there are a number of methods which utilize confidence based reward\line bonuses to encourage exploration in order to ultimately behave near optimally [KS02, Kak03, SLW+06, LH14,\line DB15, SS10, AOM17].}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
Count-based Models & Directed Exploration: There are a host of recent empirical success using\line deep RL methods which encourage exploration in some form[MKS+15, SHM+16]. The approaches which\line encourage exploration are based on a few related ideas: that of encouraging encouraging exploration through\line state visitation frequencies (e.g. [OBvdOM17, BSO+16, THF+17]) and those based on a intrinsic reward\line signal derived from novelty or prediction error [LLTyO12, PAED17, SRM+18, FCRL17, MJR15, HCC+16,\line WRR+17], aligning an intrinsic reward to the target objective [Kae93, CBS05, SLBS10, SLB09, ZOS18], or\line sample based approaches to tracking of value function uncertainty [OBPVR16, OAC18].}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
Intrinsic Learning: Works in [CBS05, SLB09, SLBS10] established computational theories of intrinsic\line reward signals (and how it might help with downstream learning of tasks) and other works also showed how\line to incorporate intrinsic rewards (in the absence of any true reward signal) [WdWK+18, BESK18, BEP+18,\line NPD+18]. The potential benefit is that such learning may help the agent reach a variety of achievable goals\line and do well on other extrinsically defined tasks, not just the task under which it was explicitly trained for\line under one specific reward function (e.g. see [CBS05, SLB09, WdWK+18, NPD+18]).}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
