Mixtures of stationary policies: Given a sequence of k policies C = (\u960\'3f0, . . . \u960\'3fk\u8722\'3f1), and \u945\'3f \u8712\'3f \u8710\'3fk,\line we define \u960\'3fmix = (\u945\'3f,C) to be a mixture over stationary policies. The (non-stationary) policy \u960\'3fmix is one\line where, at the first timestep t = 0, we sample policy \u960\'3fi with probability \u945\'3fi and then use this policy for all\line subsequent timesteps. In particular, the behavior of a mixture \u960\'3fmix with respect to an MDP is that it induces\line infinite-length trajectories \u964\'3f = (s0, a0, s1, a1 . . . ) with the probability law :}
