\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\fi0\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\ltrpar{\loch
Note that such a distribution over policies need not be representable as a stationary stochastic policy (even if\line the \u960\'3fi\u8217\'92s are stationary) due to that the sampled actions are no longer conditionally independent given the\line states.}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
3 The Objective: MaxEnt Exploration\line As each policy induces a distribution over states, we can associate a concave reward functional R(\u183\'b7) with this\line induced distribution. We say that a policy \u960\'3f\u8727\'2a is a maximum-entropy exploration policy, also to referred to as\line the max-ent policy, if the corresponding induced state distribution has the maximum possible R(d\u960\'3f) among\line the class of all policies. Lemma 3.3 assures us that the search over the class of stationary policies is sufficient.}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
\u960\'3f\u8727\'2a \u8712\'3f arg max\line \u960\'3f}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
R(d\u960\'3f).}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
Our goal is to find a policy that induces a state distribution with a comparable value of the reward functional.}
\par \pard\plain \s21\loch\sb0\sa283\ql\sb0\sa283\ltrpar{\loch
